### What is aggregation vs composition?

The lifecycle is the key differentiator.

### What does aggregation look like?

```java
package com.bank.model;

import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.List;

public class Branch {
    private String branchCode;
    private List<Employee> employees = new ArrayList<>();

    public void addEmployee(Employee emp) { employees.add(emp); }
    public void removeEmployee(Employee emp) { employees.remove(emp); }

    public List<Employee> close() {
        List<Employee> surviving = new ArrayList<>(employees);
        employees.clear();
        return surviving; // employees survive branch closure
    }
}
// Employee exists independently. Created outside Branch. Survives Branch deletion.
```

### What does composition look like?

```java
package com.bank.model;

import java.math.BigDecimal;
import java.math.RoundingMode;
import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.List;

public class Loan {
    private String loanId;
    private BigDecimal principal;
    private final List<EMI> emis = new ArrayList<>();

    public Loan(String loanId, BigDecimal principal, int tenureMonths, BigDecimal rate) {
        this.loanId = loanId;
        this.principal = principal;
        generateEMIs(tenureMonths, rate); // Loan creates EMIs
    }

    private void generateEMIs(int months, BigDecimal rate) {
        BigDecimal emiAmount = calculateEMI(principal, rate, months);
        for (int i = 1; i <= months; i++) {
            emis.add(new EMI(loanId + "-EMI-" + i, i, emiAmount));
        }
    }

    public void close() {
        emis.clear(); // EMIs die with the loan
    }
}
// EMI cannot exist alone. Created by Loan. Destroyed with Loan.
```

### How do they compare?

| Aspect | Aggregation | Composition |
|--------|------------|-------------|
| Lifecycle | Independent | Bound to parent |
| Child survives parent? | Yes | No |
| Who creates child? | External | Parent |
| UML | Hollow diamond | Filled diamond |
| JPA | No cascade | cascade ALL + orphanRemoval |
| Example | Branch has Employees | Loan owns EMIs |

### What does the JPA mapping look like?

```java
// Aggregation: no cascade delete
@Entity
public class Branch {
    @OneToMany // no cascade
    private List<Employee> employees;
}

// Composition: cascade ALL + orphanRemoval
@Entity
public class Loan {
    @OneToMany(mappedBy = "loan", cascade = javax.persistence.CascadeType.ALL, orphanRemoval = true)
    private List<EMI> emis;
}
```

### When do you use each?

Aggregation: child has independent lifecycle, can belong to multiple parents, created outside the parent. Composition: child has no meaning without parent, created by the parent, belongs to exactly one parent.

### What are common mistakes?

The most common mistake is using aggregation when composition is correct, leaving orphan rows in the database.

### What should a senior engineer know?

- In Domain-Driven Design, the Aggregate Root pattern is composition: the root entity owns all child entities.
- In microservices, composition maps to data owned by one service; aggregation maps to references across service boundaries.
